<article class="env-lemma" id="07S4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07S4">Lemma <span data-tag="07S4">66.6.5</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$. If there exists a quasi-separated scheme $U$ and a surjective étale morphism $U \to X$ such that either of the projections $U \times _ X U \to U$ is quasi-compact, then $X$ is quasi-separated. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We may think of $X$ as an algebraic space over $\mathbf{Z}$. Consider the cartesian diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ U \times _ X U \ar[r] \ar[d]_ j &  X \ar[d]^\Delta \\  U \times U \ar[r] &  X \times X }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Since $U$ is quasi-separated the projection $U \times U \to U$ is quasi-separated (as a base change of a quasi-separated morphism of schemes, see Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01KU" data-tag="01KU">26.21.12</a>). Hence the assumption in the lemma implies $j$ is quasi-compact by Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/03GI" data-tag="03GI">26.21.14</a>. By Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/02WZ" data-tag="02WZ">65.11.4</a> we see that $\Delta $ is quasi-compact as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>